        "step_anchor": "step-1",
        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We know how to evaluate expressions like <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^3, 2^2, 2^1]</span>, and even <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^0.]</span> But what if the power is negative? How would we evaluate <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^{-1},]</span> for example?</p>",
            "readable_text": "We know how to evaluate expressions like [MATH: 2^3, 2^2, 2^1], and even [MATH: 2^0.] But what if the power is negative? How would we evaluate [MATH: 2^{-1},] for example?",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To help us with that, let&#x27;s go back to the results table for powers of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "To help us with that, let's go back to the results table for powers of [MATH: 2.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^{4}|= 16; 2^{3}|= 8; 2^{2}|= 4; 2^{1}|= 2; 2^{0}|= 1; 2^{\u2212 1}|= \u25fb?]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: 2^{4}|= 16; 2^{3}|= 8; 2^{2}|= 4; 2^{1}|= 2; 2^{0}|= 1; 2^{\u2212 1}|= \u25fb?]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>As we go down the list, the number in <span>blue</span> is divided by <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2]</span> every time. If we follow the same pattern, we find that \n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^{\\color{red}{-1}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}\\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "As we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by [MATH: 2] every time. If we follow the same pattern, we find that\n[MATH: 2^{\\color{red}{-1}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}\\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Now, <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{1}{2}]</span> is just the reciprocal of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2.]</span> So, raising a number to the power of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: -1]</span> is the <em>same</em> as finding its reciprocal! </p>",
            "readable_text": "Now, [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{2}] is just the reciprocal of [MATH: 2.] So, raising a number to the power of [MATH: -1] is the same as finding its reciprocal!",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nWe know how to evaluate expressions like [MATH: 2^3, 2^2, 2^1], and even [MATH: 2^0.] But what if the power is negative? How would we evaluate [MATH: 2^{-1},] for example?\n\nContent:\nTo help us with that, let's go back to the results table for powers of [MATH: 2.]\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2^{4}|= 16; 2^{3}|= 8; 2^{2}|= 4; 2^{1}|= 2; 2^{0}|= 1; 2^{\u2212 1}|= \u25fb?]\n\nContent:\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by [MATH: 2] every time. If we follow the same pattern, we find that\n[MATH: 2^{\\color{red}{-1}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}\\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}.]\n\nContent:\nNow, [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{2}] is just the reciprocal of [MATH: 2.] So, raising a number to the power of [MATH: -1] is the same as finding its reciprocal!"
      },
