    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWe know how to evaluate expressions like [MATH: 2^3, 2^2, 2^1], and even [MATH: 2^0.] But what if the power is negative? How would we evaluate [MATH: 2^{-1},] for example?\n\nContent:\nTo help us with that, let's go back to the results table for powers of [MATH: 2.]\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2^{4}|= 16; 2^{3}|= 8; 2^{2}|= 4; 2^{1}|= 2; 2^{0}|= 1; 2^{\u2212 1}|= \u25fb?]\n\nContent:\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by [MATH: 2] every time. If we follow the same pattern, we find that\n[MATH: 2^{\\color{red}{-1}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}\\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}.]\n\nContent:\nNow, [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{2}] is just the reciprocal of [MATH: 2.] So, raising a number to the power of [MATH: -1] is the same as finding its reciprocal!\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Raising an Integer to the Power of Negative One\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: 3^{-1}] as a fraction in its simplest form.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe expression [MATH: 3^{-1}] means the reciprocal of [MATH: 3]. Hence\n\n[MATH: 3^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{3}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 7^{\u2212 1} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(7)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(7)]\nc. [MATH: (7)/(1)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 1]\ne. [MATH: (\u2212 7)^{1}]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 11^{\u2212 1} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(11)]\nb. [MATH: 1]\nc. [MATH: 11]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 11]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(11)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Raising a Negative Integer to the Power of Negative One\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is [MATH: (-4)^{-1}?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe expression [MATH: (-4)^{-1}] means the reciprocal of [MATH: -4.] Therefore,\n\n[MATH: (-4)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{4}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is [MATH: (\u2212 3)^{\u2212 1}?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 1]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(3)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(3)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 3]\ne. [MATH: 3]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is [MATH: (\u2212 10)^{\u2212 1}?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 10]\nb. [MATH: 10]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 1]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(10)]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(10)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Raising a Fraction to the Power of Negative One\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)^{-1}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe expression [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)^{-1}] means the reciprocal of [MATH: \\dfrac{2}{3}.] Therefore,\n\n[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{3}{2}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((3)/(2))^{\u2212 1} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 (2)/(3)]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 (3)/(2)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(3)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: (2)/(3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((4)/(5))^{\u2212 1} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 (4)/(3)]\nb. [MATH: (3)/(4)]\nc. [MATH: (4)/(5)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (4)/(5)]\ne. [MATH: (5)/(4)]"
